00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.810
You make sure that other
people's apps work.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:38.630
Good.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.547
MARK CHMARNY: I see a
lot of people who raised

00:00:40.547 --> 00:00:41.857
their hands both occasions.

00:00:41.857 --> 00:00:43.190
RYAN GREGG: That's right-- yeah.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.310
And then, if you
didn't raise your hand,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.310
what are you doing here?

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:46.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:46.850
Hm.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.630
All right.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:54.220
MARK CHMARNY: All right-- so
the session is called Kubernetes

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.037
or Run Serverless Everywhere.

00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:00.120
We will get to the everywhere
portion in a minute.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.580
But I want to break down the
serverless first part a little

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.519
and tease it into the
conversation after that.

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:07.050
To a large degree
today, if you talk

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.820
to people in the community,
serverless equals function.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.650
You push some code
to your cloud.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.480
And you have it
execute over there.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.520
But is it really the case?

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.550
Is serverless only a function
as a service offering?

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.170
That's the question
I'm posing to you.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.750
I think, to a large degree,
the properties of serverless

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.440
are pretty commonly
understood today.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.823
And they break down into
two separate things.

00:01:33.823 --> 00:01:35.490
First of all, is the
operational model--

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.760
how you run serverless--
maybe not develop,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.730
but run the serverless.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:43.110
So first of all, you shouldn't
have to worry about the servers

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:43.620
underneath.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:44.910
It's right in a name.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.178
I don't think we can
argue about that.

00:01:47.178 --> 00:01:48.720
The other thing is
that it also comes

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.430
with a certain level
of trust, which

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.640
means, when you push your code
and it executes on a framework

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.958
that you do not
provide, that there

00:01:55.958 --> 00:01:58.500
is a certain amount of security
that's integrated and managed

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.000
for you.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.250
So you don't have to
worry about that either.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.010
And you're paying
only for when you

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.590
use that service, when you're
actually doing something.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:12.145
More importantly, you do not pay
when it's not doing something.

00:02:12.145 --> 00:02:14.520
So when a code just sleeps
there and not doing anything--

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.090
you shouldn't pay for that.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.600
So that's the operational model.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.700
In the programming model--

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.210
the developer experience on
a serverless platform-- we

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.300
ideally have a service-based
developing model.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.040
So you call APIs.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.290
You don't run bunch
of scripts that you

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.720
have to move over and define
a bunch of environment

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.130
variables and all that.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.060
And it's event-driven.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.250
So you don't write
all kinds of code

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.890
to keep watching-- is
the file there yet?

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.030
Is the file there yet?

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.330
Is the file there yet?

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.290
But you actually are
subscribing to an event.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.730
Tell me when the file arrives.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.070
And I will fire something.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.930
These five are pretty
well understood.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.170
And most people are agreed--

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.735
those properties are serverless.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:02.180
What I would like to
add in here is one more.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.460
And that's portability.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.380
And I think, because
you're in a session titled,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.330
"Runs Serverless
Everywhere," I assume,

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.100
portability is important to you.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.890
So portability is
more than just being

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.790
able to take the code that
you have built in one platform

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.350
and able to take
it somewhere else

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.030
and run it on another platform.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.018
It's about being able to expect
the same developer experience

00:03:24.018 --> 00:03:25.310
on each one of those platforms.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.740
So for example, the
Cloud Run service

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.090
that we announced this week--

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.210
Cloud Run that it's
managed and hosted

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.730
and Cloud Run that runs in GKE--

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.120
the same developer experience.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.640
So it's not only
being able to move

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.160
this from one platform,
one operator, to another,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.440
but being able to
depend and being

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.930
able to benefit
from the innovation

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.020
that that cloud service
provider offers for you.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.640
And the other important
aspect of being portability

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.467
is that you don't
have to make a choice.

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:57.050
Day one, when you
start thinking-- you

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.250
have this great idea.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.840
And you're going
to write some code.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.640
You shouldn't have to,
at that point, say,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.020
I'm going to write code that's
going to run on the platform x.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:09.020
You should be able to use your
idiomatic developer experience.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.400
Idiomatic is just a
fancy word for natural.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.900
You don't have to
learn any special.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.600
The way you develop in Go
or node.js applications

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.550
right now is the
very same way you

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.940
should be able to develop those
applications for serverless.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.970
Now, that means
also that you should

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.670
be able to preserve
the investment.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.220
There are technologies nowadays
coming and going every month.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.210
And if we ask you to learn
yet another framework or yet

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:43.147
another set of APIs that
may be here tomorrow or not,

00:04:43.147 --> 00:04:44.480
it doesn't preserve your skills.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.810
So you have to continuously
learn new skills.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.190
With serverless, we're
hoping that the experience is

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.090
so portable that not only the
technology and the tooling

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.960
is portable to
another environment,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.080
but you actually are
able to take your skills

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.840
and be very productive
in another platform,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.670
because the set of
experiences that

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.970
are exposed to you as a
developer are one and the same.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.870
So I think, at this point, we
would agree that serverless

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.760
is bigger than just a function.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.670
And I would pose, at this point,
that serverless is also bigger

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.770
than just compute.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.800
If you look today at GCP--

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.140
at the portfolio of serverless
offerings on Google,

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:25.400
they span from everything from
data and analytics products,

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.250
machine learning--
and some call it AI.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.260
Or message queuing
is serverless.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.000
Or even the developer
experience--

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.863
so DevOps tooling is serverless.

00:05:33.863 --> 00:05:35.780
And you might have seen
some of that yesterday

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:40.190
during the keynote in
Developer evening session.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.060
So bigger than function,
bigger than compute--

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.240
and able to benefit and comprise
them with the same developer

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.010
experience.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:51.050
All right-- I want
to ask Ryan now

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.656
to talk about serverless
on Kubernetes.

00:05:53.656 --> 00:05:55.170
RYAN GREGG: Thanks, Mark.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.650
So what about serverless
on Kubernetes?

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.250
That's an interesting idea.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.070
First of all, I think
everybody in here--

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.423
because you're Kubernetes
operators or developers--

00:06:04.423 --> 00:06:06.840
except for those of you who I
don't know why you're here--

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.750
understand that Kubernetes is
really the de facto platform

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.770
now for running containers
on the cloud, on prem,

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.620
anywhere that you want
to take those workloads.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.510
In fact, there's
a giant ecosystem

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.050
with pretty much every cloud
provider, major and minor,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:25.650
participating in running a
certified Kubernetes instance.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.800
Kubernetes has really taken
off over the last four years

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.110
and become a de facto way of
doing container orchestration.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.540
But what does it really look
like when you break it down?

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.900
Kubernetes, for
operators-- it's done a lot

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.400
to improve the
operator experience

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.410
for running containers.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.170
It's taking care of the needs
of scheduling and lifecycle,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:49.080
discovery, load
balancing, storage.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.600
All the things that you had
to worry about and manage

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.030
and operate on your
own before, Kubernetes

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.960
has done a lot to make that
easier, more straightforward,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.415
and simpler.

00:07:00.415 --> 00:07:01.915
Did I just say
simple in Kubernetes?

00:07:01.915 --> 00:07:03.600
Hm.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.420
Anyway, there's a broad
number of features

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.760
that pull into this all these
great things that you can do.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.920
And the really great thing
that Kubernetes has done

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.930
is it's created
this even platform

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.623
across all the different
environments where you can run.

00:07:16.623 --> 00:07:18.040
So the learnings
and the knowledge

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.420
that you adopt as part
of using Kubernetes

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:25.000
apply whether you're running
Kubernetes on Google,

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.810
Kubernetes on prem, or
somewhere else in another cloud.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:30.190
For the developer though--

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:32.170
developers really just
want to write code.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:35.510
That's the core business
value that they provide.

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:37.495
But if you're building
on top of Kubernetes,

00:07:37.495 --> 00:07:39.620
there's all these other
things that you have to do.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:41.860
You have to containerize
your application.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.068
You have to figure out how
to upload that application

00:07:44.068 --> 00:07:45.160
into a container registry.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.785
You have to actually deploy the
service, create the deployment,

00:07:47.785 --> 00:07:51.127
and all the underlying objects
inside the Kubernetes API.

00:07:51.127 --> 00:07:53.710
You then have to figure out how
to expose that to the internet

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.840
or to your service
mesh or whatever

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:58.480
the ingress gateway that
you're going to be using is.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.490
You have to set up
logging and monitoring

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:01.972
and deal with all
of these aspects.

00:08:01.972 --> 00:08:04.180
And then you have to figure
out how to properly scale

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.170
your workload.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.730
You might have an
I/O bound workload

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.860
where you can put a lot
of concurrency into it.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.510
Or you might have a
CPU-bound workload,

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:14.620
where you need to do
more horizontal scaling.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:16.330
There's a lot there
and a lot more

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.250
of it than just writing code.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.220
So earlier this week,
we introduced Cloud Run,

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:24.100
which is this concept
of bringing serverless

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:26.290
to containers and
trying to remove

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.580
a bunch of the details
of the infrastructure

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.150
and what underlies
what it takes to run

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.860
one of these containers
in the cloud.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.970
And Cloud Run is available
as a fully-hosted service.

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:39.855
So you don't have the
Kubernetes aspect.

00:08:39.855 --> 00:08:41.230
You don't have to
worry about any

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.659
of the details of what
cluster it's running in,

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:45.070
how it's horizontally scaled.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.980
You just provide the
container into Google Cloud.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.640
And we go run it for you, giving
you a URL that you can access,

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:52.840
giving you SSL--

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:55.755
all of the benefits of running
on a serverless platform.

00:08:55.755 --> 00:08:56.380
Which is great.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.810
But all of you aren't running
on a serverless platform.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:00.280
You're running on Kubernetes.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.290
So we didn't want
to leave you out.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.720
So we also brought
Cloud Run on GKE,

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.130
which is the same
great Cloud Run

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.560
experience for the
developer, but running

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.330
on top of Kubernetes engine.

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:13.180
So it gives you the flexibility
to continue the Kubernetes

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:14.860
investment that
you've already made

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:16.720
but to get all of the
benefits of serverless

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.280
for your developers.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.360
Things like automatic
scale, easy deployment,

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.790
network programming, and
automatic participation

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.410
in the service mesh
are all features

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:28.817
provided by Cloud Run on GKE.

00:09:28.817 --> 00:09:31.150
It also integrates with all
of the Kubernetes features--

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:35.095
so using policy management--
is that what it's called?

00:09:35.095 --> 00:09:37.930
I can never remember the
name of that feature, which

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.370
we announced this week.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.207
The ability to
manage these things--

00:09:41.207 --> 00:09:42.790
everything that you
do with Kubernetes

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:44.920
today applies to
Cloud Run on GKE,

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.710
because it's running on top of
your existing infrastructure.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:49.210
It also means you
can take advantage

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:53.710
of the features of Kubernetes
on GKE, like custom nodes,

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:55.600
being able to bring in
custom machine sizes,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.840
being able to access hardware
accelerators, like GPUs

00:09:58.840 --> 00:09:59.830
and TPUs.

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:01.330
All of the advanced
compute features

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:03.970
that are available in the
Kubernetes platform that

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:06.470
aren't necessarily available
on a serverless platform

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:08.290
are yours on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:14.720
So we now have the ability
to offer serverless anywhere,

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.210
between a fully
serverless platform

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.172
that's managed
through Cloud Run,

00:10:19.172 --> 00:10:20.630
where everything
is hidden from you

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:22.670
and you don't have to
worry about the details,

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:25.610
to Cloud Run on GKE, where
we manage the control plane

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:27.710
and provide the same
developer experience,

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:31.137
but it runs inside Kubernetes
Engine, and Knative,

00:10:31.137 --> 00:10:32.720
which is the open
source platform that

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.520
makes all of this possible--

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:37.760
which allows you to run the same
API, the same control plane,

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:41.160
on any Kubernetes
installation anywhere.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.340
So you can have the same
serverless experience

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.440
across the board,
from fully managed

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.530
all the way to do-it-yourself
with open source.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.990
So let's step back and take
a look at this picture again.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:57.120
With Cloud Run on top of
GKE, now developers really

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.710
can just focus on
writing their code.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.348
They can have that serverless
developer experience

00:11:01.348 --> 00:11:03.390
and still run it on top
of all of your Kubernetes

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:04.340
infrastructure.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.060
And they don't need
to worry about all

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.610
of those other concerns, so
they can be more productive

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:09.360
and get more done.

00:11:12.808 --> 00:11:14.350
Let's go a little
bit deeper though--

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:15.910
because this is a
200-level talk--

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.370
and talk about-- what are the
actual benefits that you get

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:20.530
out of using Cloud Run on GKE?

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:24.010
So when I deploy my container
on Cloud Run on GKE,

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:26.380
using the serverless
infrastructure on Kubernetes

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:30.250
Engine, I get my container
automatically scaled,

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.050
including scaling to 0.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:33.820
So we look at the
HTTP traffic that

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:35.470
is coming into this service.

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:37.693
And if there isn't any
after a period of time,

00:11:37.693 --> 00:11:38.860
the container will time-out.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.390
We'll spin down the pod.

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:43.320
And it's taking up no
resources inside your cluster.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.370
When a request
comes in, it lands

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:48.040
on an activator component,
which understands

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.080
which service definitions
we need to go spin up.

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:52.030
We'll create a pod
or multiple pods,

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.190
depending on how much
traffic is coming in,

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.260
and start routing the
connections directly

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.760
to those instances.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.860
And we'll scale up
from 1, obviously,

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.267
to as many as it takes to
serve the load that you have.

00:12:02.267 --> 00:12:03.850
And there are some
parameters that you

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.770
can use to fine-tune
how that scaling works.

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:09.520
We also offer managed
configurations and revisions.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.470
So we keep track
of every revision

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:15.130
of a service that is deployed
through Cloud Run on GKE.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:16.870
And you can easily
move between them.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:18.770
You can roll back
to an older version.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.950
Say, you deploy something
new and there's an error.

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:23.480
You can quickly revert back.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:25.540
We also ensure that,
while we're moving over

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.707
to the new revision,
there's no downtime.

00:12:27.707 --> 00:12:29.290
Requests that are
coming in seamlessly

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:32.670
get migrated from one instance
in one version to the next one,

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.630
so your service can
remain live in production,

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.763
as you deploy a new version.

00:12:36.763 --> 00:12:38.680
We also integrate with
the Istio service mesh,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:40.180
because we're running
on Kubernetes.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.700
So every service that's deployed
through Cloud Run on GKE

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.500
is a member of the service mesh.

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.560
And you can choose to expose
that service either externally,

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.810
outside the cluster, or
only on the service mesh,

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:51.445
if you want to do
an internal service.

00:12:51.445 --> 00:12:53.670
And Mark's going to do a
really cool demo about that

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.137
in a little bit.

00:12:55.137 --> 00:12:57.220
And then another great
benefit of Cloud Run on GKE

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:00.160
is that it enables the custom
domain story and certificate

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:01.840
management story on
top of the cluster

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:03.550
for serverless services.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:06.620
So we automatically generate
URLs for those services.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:08.600
That can be on a
custom domain name,

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.170
so that you don't have to
worry about all of the plumbing

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:12.920
to make that happen.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.510
And then, of course, Cloud Run
on GKE is built on top of GKE.

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:19.750
So it can connect into a
bunch of great GCP services,

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:21.880
like Cloud Build
and Stackdriver,

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:23.500
for doing logging
and monitoring.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.318
But it's also a
pluggable infrastructure.

00:13:25.318 --> 00:13:27.610
So if you want to extend it
with a partner integration,

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.740
like Datadog, you can
do that, if that's

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.210
what your business depends on.

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:33.400
Or you can use GitLab
as the CI/CD system.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:34.900
And we'll show you
a little bit more

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:36.650
about how that works
here in a little bit.

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:42.350
So let me give you a demo of
what this actually looks like--

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:45.560
just to get a little bit
more real than me talking.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:48.806
So if we could
switch to demo 1--

00:13:48.806 --> 00:13:49.880
aha.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:51.380
Here I am in Cloud Console.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.028
This probably looks very
familiar to everybody here.

00:13:54.028 --> 00:13:55.820
And I'm going to go
into Kubernetes Engine,

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.070
just to show you what it
looks like to stand this up.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:03.450
So I will create a cluster
and wait for my UI to load.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.130
And I'm going to
go ahead and create

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.560
a highly-available
cluster, because that's

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:10.140
what you should be using if
you have production workloads.

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.240
And I'm going to scroll
down to the bottom.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.360
And I'm going to turn
on Stackdriver logging

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:16.140
and monitoring.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.300
I'm going to turn on Istio.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:20.328
And I'm going to turn
on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:14:20.328 --> 00:14:22.120
And I'm not actually
going to click Create,

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:24.210
because it takes a little
bit too long for a demo.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.325
But that's how easy it is to
stand up a cluster that Cloud

00:14:27.325 --> 00:14:28.960
Run on GKE can run on.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:31.210
Today, you can only do that
when you create a cluster.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:32.877
But very soon, we'll
give you the option

00:14:32.877 --> 00:14:35.690
of doing that on an
existing cluster as well.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.620
And once I have it
running, I can move over

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:39.930
to the Cloud Run UI now.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.850
And this is really the
developer experience

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:44.355
for deploying applications
into this environment.

00:14:44.355 --> 00:14:46.230
If you think of the
Kubernetes Engine control

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:49.590
pane as being the operator
experience in Kubernetes,

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.660
Cloud Run gives you the
developer experience

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.680
on top of Cloud Run on GKE.

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:56.790
And I can just click
to create a service.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:00.346
I can browse to find a
container image, in this case.

00:15:00.346 --> 00:15:02.326
I'll pick this one.

00:15:02.326 --> 00:15:05.320
It seems like the
right shaw to me.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:07.720
And I will choose to put this
into my Kubernetes Engine

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:11.572
cluster, instead of into
the managed namespace.

00:15:11.572 --> 00:15:13.030
And I'll just use
Default. I'll say

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:14.697
that I want it to be
exposed externally,

00:15:14.697 --> 00:15:17.860
so that I can actually show you
what service I have deployed.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:19.530
And I'll click Create.

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.750
And what this is doing right
now is using the Knative API

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:26.150
to go out and define a Knative
service on this cluster.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:27.760
And under the covers,
Knative is doing

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.310
all of the work necessary
to create the activator,

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:33.970
manage the routing, program
Istio to enable the URL that I

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:37.240
have here to work, and
making the whole system up

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:37.930
and running.

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:40.330
And now, you can see--
my revision is deployed

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:43.080
and ready for me to activate.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:43.730
Click on that.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.920
And you can see,
here's my PDF service.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:47.920
If you came to the developer
keynote last night,

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:50.780
you'll be like, hey,
I saw this before.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:52.630
But this is just a
microservice that

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:55.100
allows you to convert a
Word document into a PDF--

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:56.230
a very simple.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.980
You can see that it's
running on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:02.830
I can actually go back and
deploy the same service

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:05.120
onto the hosted Cloud
Run experience--

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:06.930
the managed Cloud Run.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.700
And you'll see, it's
exactly the same experience

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:10.670
as I had before.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:14.270
But this time, I'm going to pick
one of these Cloud Run regions.

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:17.950
And well-- make sure
I check this box.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:20.680
This is deploying it into
the fully managed serverless

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.772
experience, where there
is no GKE cluster.

00:16:22.772 --> 00:16:24.355
There's nothing
actually backing this.

00:16:24.355 --> 00:16:28.360
And you see-- the developer
experience is the same.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:29.870
So all that is really cool.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:31.390
But what about Knative?

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.570
What about being able to run
the service in other places

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:35.660
as well?

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:38.740
So I'm going to show the
last step of this demo, which

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:43.000
is actually using the
Knative API, the underlying

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:47.625
open source, on an IBM cloud
cluster, as just an example.

00:16:47.625 --> 00:16:49.750
It could have been any
Kubernetes cluster anywhere.

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:52.280
But I happen to have one
of those easily available.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:17:00.545
So I'm going to use Gcloud
Run to get my service yaml--

00:17:00.545 --> 00:17:01.920
PDF service.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:05.980
And you'll see, here's
the Knative API yaml

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:07.119
for my service.

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:09.190
If I scroll up,
you'll see, there's

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:10.520
a bunch of metadata, of course.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.220
But the spec is pretty simple.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:15.609
It's just saying to run the
latest version of this revision

00:17:15.609 --> 00:17:17.710
and for this particular image.

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:21.680
This is the Knative
API, very simply.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:24.940
And I've actually put
this into service.yaml,

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:26.470
just to show that I'm not lying.

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:30.760
And I will do kubectl.apply
into my other cluster,

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:32.770
the service.yaml.

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.780
And this is going
to do the same thing

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.850
that the UI was doing before--

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:40.660
create this Knative service
on this Kubernetes cluster.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:42.820
And I can do, Get K Service.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.580
And you'll see, that service
is now up and running.

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:50.550
I have a URL that I can access,
from this other service.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:52.120
And thanks to the
power of Knative,

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:53.900
I have the same thing running.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:56.230
So we have the same
portability of the experience

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.150
that normal Kubernetes
would give you.

00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:00.567
But I didn't have to worry
about any of the details of how

00:18:00.567 --> 00:18:03.280
to program Kubernetes,
how to make it auto scale,

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.980
how to do all of those
developer complexity things

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:07.400
that I didn't want to do before.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.940
Knative, Cloud Run,
and Cloud Run on GKE

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.300
have taken care of that.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:14.033
So as a developer, I can
just focus on writing my code

00:18:14.033 --> 00:18:16.450
and not be concerned with all
of the other details of what

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:17.976
I have to worry about.

00:18:17.976 --> 00:18:22.250
If we could switch back
to the slides, please--

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:26.450
so to give you a little bit
more example of how all of this

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:27.520
works in action--

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.020
you need to give
your thing to him--

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:30.710
MARK CHMARNY: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:32.710
RYAN GREGG: I'd like to
bring up Daniel Gruesso,

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:35.180
from GitLab, to show you
how GitLab has integrated

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.820
with Cloud Run on
GKE and Knative

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:42.530
to provide a portable
experience for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:46.790
DANIEL GRUESSO:
Hello-- hello-- hello.

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:47.716
Hi.

00:18:47.716 --> 00:18:49.110
So thank you, Ryan.

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.630
So at GitLab, we
couldn't agree more.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.280
Kubernetes is the
de facto platform

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.170
for running containers.

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:56.813
And we've worked
to integrate all

00:18:56.813 --> 00:18:58.230
of those workflows
in our product.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.690
So serverless is no different.

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:07.540
We believe Knative
is an important part

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.820
of enabling the
development experience

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:12.340
and basically really
allowing developers

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:16.770
to focus on writing code and
applications and adding value

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:19.270
and not having to
focus on maintaining

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:23.150
infrastructure and configuring
it and all of those things.

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:25.450
So as you know, GitLab
provides a unified experience

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:28.510
for operators and
developers, where

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:31.000
you can manage both your
code and your infrastructure

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:32.290
from a single app.

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:33.910
So there's a lot
of power in that.

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:36.070
So let's see what
that's all about.

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:37.150
Can we switch to demo 2?

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:46.080
All right-- so here I
have my GitLab project.

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:50.220
Let's head over to
operations and Kubernetes.

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:54.000
So I see here that the operator
has set up a Kubernetes cluster

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:55.290
on my project.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:58.380
And Cloud Run on GKE
has been enabled here.

00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:00.690
I see that the domain that
I requested for my app

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:04.215
has been configured-- in
this case, GitLabCloud.run.

00:20:04.215 --> 00:20:06.840
So here's where the benefits of
GitLab start to become visible.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.960
Here, I see that,
with a single click,

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:15.210
an operator can configure that
domain to the Istio ingress

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:16.380
end-point.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.660
And behind the scenes,
what's that's doing is

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:21.270
it's adding the
domain to config map

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:25.710
to resolve all the requests to
the service, using this domain.

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:27.780
So I didn't have to
expose any configuration

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:28.830
on Kubernetes-- anything.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:30.190
It's a single click op.

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:31.500
So that's really great.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.280
Let's move onto
the project here.

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:35.730
And let's take a look.

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:38.790
And let's take a look at
how easy it is to deploy

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:41.838
a service on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:20:41.838 --> 00:20:44.130
So there are two types of
services that I could deploy.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:46.010
I could deploy a
function as a service.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:48.240
Or I could deploy a
full serverless app.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:52.350
And Knative will scale it up
and down, based on the requests.

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:55.980
So for this demo, I will go
ahead and deploy a function.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:57.400
So I only need two templates.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.670
The first one is
the GitLabCI.yaml.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:04.680
And basically, this contains a
reference to the Cloud Run CLI

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:05.660
that we're using.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:08.640
That will take care of building
and deploying your Cloud Run

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:10.230
service.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:12.630
And then the second template
is the serverless.yaml.

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:16.180
So I only need this one if I'm
going to deal with functions.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:19.110
And here, basically, it provides
information about my functions,

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:22.830
such as the name, description,
the runtime that I'm using.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:26.670
In this case, it's going to be
a TriggerMesh node.js runtime.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:27.430
And that's it.

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:31.088
So that's really all
I need to get started.

00:21:31.088 --> 00:21:32.880
So the very next thing
that I'm going to do

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.530
is I'm going to
add the files that

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:37.210
are specific to my function.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:40.450
So here, I'm just going
to add three files.

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:42.810
The first file is echo.js.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:45.990
This is a simple function that
will echo back whatever input

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:47.200
I give to it.

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:50.040
And the other two
files are JSON files

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:53.248
that hold the metadata
for my service.

00:21:53.248 --> 00:21:53.790
So that's it.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:55.623
Let's go ahead and add
those to the project.

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:06.967
Let's give this a commit message
here at echo function files--

00:22:06.967 --> 00:22:07.961
and push.

00:22:11.732 --> 00:22:14.190
All right-- so let's make sure
that all those files made it

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:15.790
back to my repo properly.

00:22:15.790 --> 00:22:18.240
Great-- I see that the Echo
directory made it here.

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:21.420
And it contains all of the
files that I just pushed.

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:23.970
So this, in turn, will
create a CI pipeline

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:26.670
that will actually take care
of building and deploying

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:28.180
the Knative service.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:30.730
So it takes about a
minute to complete.

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:33.520
So let me show you what it
looks like when it's done.

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:36.450
Here, I can see
the whole process

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:39.360
in a single stage of
building and deploying

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:42.130
the Cloud Run on GKE service.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:44.280
So when this happens,
this will populate

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:47.240
an entry in my serverless page.

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:48.990
So that's under
Operations and Serverless.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.600
And the entry on
this page is going

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:55.720
to be a reference to
the Knative service.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:57.570
So here, as soon
as it loads, we'll

00:22:57.570 --> 00:23:00.075
see a couple of things that
are specific to the service.

00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:06.820
All right-- so I see here,
the service name, the service

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:08.950
description, and
the end point where

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:11.450
the service is being served.

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:12.140
And that's it.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:14.770
So that means that my
service is now out there.

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:18.340
And I should be able to
send the requests to it.

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:19.510
And hopefully, it will work.

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.880
And it will echo back
whatever input I give to it.

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:25.840
So for this first test,
let's give it this input,

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:28.390
Hello from Google Next 2019.

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:29.870
Here, I'm just
going to use curl.

00:23:29.870 --> 00:23:32.830
So you see the content
type, the request type,

00:23:32.830 --> 00:23:33.920
and the end point.

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:35.170
So let's go ahead and do that.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:41.402
So here, the service
is scaling from 0.

00:23:41.402 --> 00:23:43.610
So it's going to spin up
whatever resources it needs.

00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:46.560
And I see that it
echoed back this data.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:48.650
So let's try it a
second time here.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:51.050
This time, I'll pass it
GitLab serverless functions

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:54.325
on Cloud Run for GKE.

00:23:54.325 --> 00:23:55.700
So you see that
that second time,

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:58.220
it was a lot faster, because
the resources have already

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:00.080
been spun up.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:03.470
And then basically, as you
give it load, it will scale up.

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:06.680
And when there's no load, it
will scale back down to 0.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:09.950
So here, if we click on my
service, I'll see more detail.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:12.470
I see-- because this is
running on Cloud Run for GKE--

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:14.720
there's a Kubernetes cluster.

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:16.780
And I will see the
scale for my functions.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:20.390
So in this case, I see that
it's using only one pod.

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:22.460
As you give it more
traffic, you'll

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:24.590
see more pods spun up here.

00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:26.870
And very soon, we'll add
the number of invocations

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:28.890
that you have for
your functions.

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:31.700
So as you can see, it was very
simple to deploy a service

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:33.020
from 0.

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:35.090
And while it's
still early, you can

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:36.775
see that there's a
lot of power in that.

00:24:36.775 --> 00:24:38.150
And like Ryan
said, we don't have

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:43.070
to concern ourselves with
being experts in Kubernetes.

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:45.830
And this is being added
to GitLab CE, which

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:47.440
is open source.

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:50.150
So you can feel free to
contribute any use cases

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:52.860
that you may need on top of it.

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:54.200
And that's all I have.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:55.718
Back to you, Mark.

00:24:55.718 --> 00:24:57.594
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:59.939 --> 00:25:04.620
MARK CHMARNY: I'm going
to switch back to slides.

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:07.110
Daniel walked us through
some of the benefits

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:09.720
and the overview of
the demo and gave us

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:12.720
a good idea of how we can
partner, using the Knative

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:16.530
APIs that are very open, with
a broader number of partners

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:18.660
in the community that enable--

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:20.963
whether it's CI/CD,
whether it's logging,

00:25:20.963 --> 00:25:22.380
whether all the
extra capabilities

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:24.180
that you might
potentially depend on

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:26.070
when you were
developing on premise

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:29.743
and how you want to use them in
a more distributed environment,

00:25:29.743 --> 00:25:32.160
whether it's in a cloud or
continue developing on premise,

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:35.310
but using some of the
technologies that Cloud Run or,

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:37.582
specifically, Knative enable.

00:25:37.582 --> 00:25:38.790
So what I'm going to do now--

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:46.080
I'm going to go in and maybe
highlight another use case that

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:49.130
captures some of the
more real-life scenarios,

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:53.033
as when you're developing a more
microservice-based solution.

00:25:53.033 --> 00:25:54.450
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:57.660
going to start with a very
simplistic use case, which

00:25:57.660 --> 00:26:02.830
is a single service deployed
on Cloud Run that exposes

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:07.080
a REST interface to the outside
world that can be invoked

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:11.820
with an image that then it's
turning around and calling

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:17.130
a Google Cloud Vision API to
get an identification of a logo

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:20.062
that might be included
in that image.

00:26:20.062 --> 00:26:21.270
What we're going to do then--

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:22.468
we're going to say, OK.

00:26:22.468 --> 00:26:23.010
That's great.

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:25.020
Except now, everybody
in the world

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:29.550
is able to run my credits
against Vision API, which

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:32.800
is something I probably don't
want to enable too much.

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:35.460
So we're going to front this
with yet another microservice,

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:37.410
which is going to
provide authentication,

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:42.450
using Google OAuth service,
and expose a simple UI, also,

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:46.130
so that users can interact
with that API much easier.

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:48.630
That's great-- except, if you're
paying attention right now,

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:51.390
you're quickly realizing
that both of those services

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:53.410
are exposed to
the outside world.

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:54.955
So you probably
all say, ha ha ha.

00:26:54.955 --> 00:26:56.080
I can just go around there.

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:58.450
And I can continue calling
my original service.

00:26:58.450 --> 00:26:59.950
And everything is
going to be great.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:04.408
So let's see how
services on Cloud Run

00:27:04.408 --> 00:27:06.450
can help you to deal with
this kind of situation.

00:27:06.450 --> 00:27:07.770
Can we switch it to demo 1?

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:22.880
All right-- so I have
a simple image here.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:24.120
You and I know what it is.

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:25.910
I've seen a lot of
hands raised initially,

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:28.077
when we kicked off the
session, talking about people

00:27:28.077 --> 00:27:29.270
who know what Kubernetes is.

00:27:29.270 --> 00:27:31.860
So you probably know what it is.

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:38.388
But when a machine asks
and looks at that image,

00:27:38.388 --> 00:27:39.430
it might know what it is.

00:27:39.430 --> 00:27:42.460
So the Vision API provides
one of its sub-services

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:44.510
that allow you to
identify logos.

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:48.070
So actually, before we
even go there, let's

00:27:48.070 --> 00:27:49.410
first of all deploy this image.

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:51.910
I wouldn't want to run a REST
service against something that

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:52.648
doesn't exist.

00:27:52.648 --> 00:27:54.190
So first of all,
we're going to run--

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:56.560
it looks a little complex,
because we're including some

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:57.740
of the additional services.

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:00.465
So Ryan has deployed
service, using UI.

00:28:00.465 --> 00:28:02.590
I'm going to show you how
to do this using Gcloud--

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:05.530
exactly the same experience,
the same variables, but just

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:07.490
different tool.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:09.670
So obviously,
Gcloud is the tool.

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:12.400
We're going to use Run,
which is the service we

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:13.730
are announcing here.

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:14.770
We're going to deploy.

00:28:14.770 --> 00:28:17.080
Here's my creative
service that, apparently,

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:19.000
always has to start with K now.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:21.340
And we're going to add a
couple additional variables.

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:23.230
We're going to deploy an image.

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:25.510
You've seen, during
the demos-- as often,

00:28:25.510 --> 00:28:26.990
for ease of demonstration--

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:29.500
deploy an image
with Latest, which

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:30.730
is great for demonstration.

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.897
You probably would not want
to do that in real-life.

00:28:32.897 --> 00:28:36.040
You want a specific hash
shot of the image that

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:40.180
represents a state of
that image, as it was,

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:44.740
that will allow you to
benefit from the auto scaling,

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:49.710
without worrying that, when
you deploy the service today

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:52.300
and an image and
the solution has

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:54.962
to scale to additional
nodes tomorrow,

00:28:54.962 --> 00:28:57.170
that you're pulling a
different version of the image.

00:28:57.170 --> 00:28:59.140
So a consistent, same
version of the image

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:00.960
is going to be on
every single service.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:01.870
And we're also
providing a couple

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:04.530
additional environment variables
in here, like a release mode.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:05.930
So let's do this.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:10.000
Now, the thing I like to point
out here during the demos is--

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:10.930
this is pretty fast.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:12.160
When you think about--

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:14.860
docker images can
get pretty large--

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:17.080
how fast you do this and
how quickly you can deploy

00:29:17.080 --> 00:29:18.590
it is pretty impressive.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:23.300
So what Cloud Run gave us
here is a specific version--

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:27.850
so the name of the service plus
some unique characters there.

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:28.900
It also gave us a URL.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:31.930
I have this entire cluster
configured with a custom domain

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:36.790
and SSL certificates, so we can
actually resemble real-- more--

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:38.210
world scenario.

00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:39.610
So let's go back to our kernel.

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:41.420
And let's run it.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:43.630
So the simple thing--

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:46.960
we're going to post a JSON
object, which is very simple.

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:48.842
It has only an ID and a URL.

00:29:48.842 --> 00:29:50.800
And then we're going to
pipe it through jQuery,

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:54.370
so we can have a little
more color over response.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:56.840
So we see here, very quickly,
the service was running.

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:59.020
It returned an identification.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.170
It identified it as Kubernetes.

00:30:02.170 --> 00:30:04.210
I'm actually underselling
the Vision API.

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:06.940
Vision API returns
a broad number

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:10.340
of set of features
of that image,

00:30:10.340 --> 00:30:13.420
including attributes,
colors, and all other things.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:15.670
I'm just extracting one
thing for ease of demo.

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:19.510
But Cloud Vision API is
pretty impressive service.

00:30:19.510 --> 00:30:23.530
OK, so what we're
going to do now is--

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:24.880
REST interface is great.

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:29.745
But where are we?

00:30:29.745 --> 00:30:31.870
Let's deploy the UI
application, so we can actually

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:32.740
take a look at it.

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:34.540
Again-- much bigger command--

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:36.610
yes, there's more environment
variables in here.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:40.670
But bottom line is we're going
to run the exact same thing.

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:43.668
And when that is done--

00:30:43.668 --> 00:30:45.960
great-- we can actually switch
to the browser and start

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:46.460
using--

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:50.850
can you guys see it?

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:53.310
Yeah-- so clearly,
an application

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:54.910
created for
demonstration purposes.

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:55.995
There's not much there.

00:30:55.995 --> 00:30:57.870
But one thing that we
can do is we can easily

00:30:57.870 --> 00:30:59.323
log in here into a service.

00:30:59.323 --> 00:31:00.990
I'm going to my
personal one, so I'm not

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:02.970
exposing any Google stuff here.

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.670
And we have a nice UI.

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:07.118
Oh, I have to go shopping.

00:31:07.118 --> 00:31:08.910
I think it's the same
t-shirt I was wearing

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:11.460
when I was taking the picture.

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:14.970
All right-- so back to
our Kubernetes image--

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:17.670
we can run this
through the same UI.

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:20.580
But like we said,
there is this problem

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:26.010
that I can continue coming back
to my curl and just running it.

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:29.900
So clearly not
working as intended.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:31.940
I'm going to come back
to this UI in here.

00:31:31.940 --> 00:31:33.000
We have this new version.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:36.660
Let's refresh this, because we
just deployed a new version.

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:39.350
So now, you see, three minutes
ago, I deployed a new one.

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.803
There's a number of features
here logging details.

00:31:41.803 --> 00:31:43.220
You see the
environment variables.

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:45.230
You can actually
see the yaml file

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:49.190
that Ryan has deployed
across IBM Cloud and ours.

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:51.980
You can assume the same thing
with environment variables.

00:31:51.980 --> 00:31:53.660
The one thing I want
to highlight here--

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:55.327
in addition to these
other capabilities,

00:31:55.327 --> 00:31:58.940
you can actually come in here
and very easily change this

00:31:58.940 --> 00:32:00.720
to an internal service.

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:03.350
So let's wait for
this to finish.

00:32:03.350 --> 00:32:06.230
Let's come over here
and, first of all,

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:10.680
clear this and maybe
do a little verbose.

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.080
All right-- so curl
is a lot more verbose.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:15.910
But the important
thing is over here.

00:32:15.910 --> 00:32:18.060
First of all, the
service was 404.

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:22.150
So it's not like we put some
kind of token or security

00:32:22.150 --> 00:32:22.650
on it.

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:24.400
We literally removed
the route to it.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:26.650
It's not accessible
from outside world.

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:28.320
The other thing you
should notice here--

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:32.220
automatically, we recognize that
this traffic supports HTTP/2.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:36.060
So we've upgraded the
service to HTTP/2.

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:38.237
So let's now see if our
UI continues working.

00:32:38.237 --> 00:32:40.320
Because if we just shut
down the back-end service,

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.530
that's probably not
what we wanted, right?

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:45.960
Now, I don't think we used
YouTube before-- yeah?

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:46.710
There it is.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:50.190
So our service is able to
use the back-end service

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:52.350
internally, inside
of the cluster,

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:55.740
by discovering its internal
URL and consuming that,

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:57.390
versus using the external.

00:32:57.390 --> 00:32:58.980
That's a great
capability that allows

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:01.280
you to start mix and
match different services.

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:03.930
And you continue benefiting
from the Istio mesh that's

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.090
running underneath to
give you telemetry,

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:08.160
access posts, your request
paths, and all that.

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:13.950
In fact, Istio request
path access pattern

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:16.544
is actually used in here to
deliver that very capability.

00:33:18.897 --> 00:33:20.480
All right-- so let's
switch to slides.

00:33:24.272 --> 00:33:29.240
So what we've done
here-- just to recap--

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:30.740
we've deployed a
number of services.

00:33:30.740 --> 00:33:33.200
Actually, going a little
further, what I've done

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:37.410
is deployed yet another service
that's providing a metering.

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:41.510
So we actually keep a state
of each user's requests.

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:45.500
And we persist them
into Firestore API,

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:47.270
so even if you were
to go to the UI

00:33:47.270 --> 00:33:49.670
and start using it
thousands of times,

00:33:49.670 --> 00:33:52.680
I will shut you down after
hundreds of queries per day.

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:54.920
So we have a number
of back-end services

00:33:54.920 --> 00:33:57.200
that are accessible
only internally.

00:33:57.200 --> 00:34:00.560
They're accessible to the
front UI as a backing service.

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:02.300
But the front UI
is the only service

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:03.500
that's exposed externally.

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:06.440
It allows users and forces them
to authenticate-- a little more

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:08.120
real-world scenario
of how you would

00:34:08.120 --> 00:34:09.312
develop these applications.

00:34:09.312 --> 00:34:11.270
And the beauty of this
is that you can continue

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:12.312
to keep building on that.

00:34:12.312 --> 00:34:16.670
You can continue adding,
using Cloud Run on GKE.

00:34:16.670 --> 00:34:19.520
You can continue
building the services.

00:34:19.520 --> 00:34:22.400
And behind the scene, if the
UI is not exposing something,

00:34:22.400 --> 00:34:24.469
you can use kubectl
to manage this,

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:28.659
as if this was normal Kubernetes
service that was deployed by--

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:34.780
so, Ryan.

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:37.280
Tell us again about-- what are
the different options we have

00:34:37.280 --> 00:34:39.380
and where we can
run these services,

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:41.089
using Knative back services?

00:34:41.089 --> 00:34:41.480
RYAN GREGG: [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:42.147
Thank you, Mark.

00:34:42.147 --> 00:34:43.935
That was, I think, a cool demo.

00:34:43.935 --> 00:34:45.864
Did everybody else think
that was a cool demo?

00:34:45.864 --> 00:34:47.684
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:49.504 --> 00:34:51.210
MARK CHMARNY:
[INAUDIBLE] to beg for--

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:52.670
RYAN GREGG: No, I'll
beg for applause.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:53.170
That's fine.

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:56.210
That's a product manager job.

00:34:56.210 --> 00:35:01.860
So serverless where you want
it with Knative and Cloud Run--

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:04.875
I want to emphasize the
portability aspect here.

00:35:04.875 --> 00:35:06.500
Because really, what
we're trying to do

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:09.710
is make it so that you can
have the developer experience

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:12.230
of serverless in any
environment where you

00:35:12.230 --> 00:35:14.600
want to enable that experience.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:17.750
We know that not everybody
fits into the mold of what

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:20.940
a fully-hosted serverless
option can offer

00:35:20.940 --> 00:35:23.870
and that there are lots of
reasons why customers want

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:28.400
to stay on GKE, on Kubernetes,
and run on Kubernetes, really,

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:28.948
anywhere.

00:35:28.948 --> 00:35:30.740
But the developer
experience shouldn't have

00:35:30.740 --> 00:35:32.390
to change as a result of that.

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:35.210
You should be able to get
the same experience for how

00:35:35.210 --> 00:35:36.380
you write code--

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:40.920
being idiomatic, as Mark
said-- my favorite word--

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:42.560
across all the
different ecosystems,

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:45.037
so that not just the
workload can be portable--

00:35:45.037 --> 00:35:47.120
like I showed, being able
to deploy the same thing

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:48.360
in multiple places--

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:50.780
but that the skills can
transfer-- that the developer

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:53.780
experience can be portable,
so that what I learned today

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:57.290
using Cloud Run on GKE
can, on my next project,

00:35:57.290 --> 00:35:59.540
be applied to Cloud Run
and, on the next project,

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:02.690
be applied to an
on-premises cluster.

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:04.910
And so we've announced
these products--

00:36:04.910 --> 00:36:08.750
Cloud Run, Cloud Run on GKE,
which are in beta today.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:11.690
I really encourage everybody to
take them out and try these out

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:13.310
today and give them a try.

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:18.720
And look at Knative as well, if
you have a Kubernetes cluster,

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:23.284
where you're interested in
running serverless workloads.

00:36:23.284 --> 00:36:26.240
Oh, I talked about this already.

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:29.490
So there's a bunch of
related sections, of course.

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:32.720
This is afternoon on Thursday,
so almost all of these

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:33.980
have already happened.

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:35.720
They're all going
to be on YouTube

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:37.950
in a matter of a few days.

00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:40.250
So please, go back
and watch them

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:44.270
if you're interested more in
Knative or in the overall Cloud

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:45.350
Run vision.

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:48.140
I do want to plug,
though, the SVR305 that's

00:36:48.140 --> 00:36:51.170
happening this afternoon,
which is a deep dive on Knative

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:51.753
eventing.

00:36:51.753 --> 00:36:53.420
So everything that
we talked about today

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:55.790
is on the serving path and
being able to auto-scale

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:57.020
your workloads.

00:36:57.020 --> 00:36:59.837
But if you're familiar with
how we define serverless--

00:36:59.837 --> 00:37:01.670
in one of the slides
that Mark talked about,

00:37:01.670 --> 00:37:04.020
there is that
event-driven piece,

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:05.840
which we didn't talk
about a whole lot

00:37:05.840 --> 00:37:08.660
today, because it's still
coming through Knative eventing.

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:10.310
And so [INAUDIBLE]
and Mark are going

00:37:10.310 --> 00:37:13.490
to be presenting both the vision
of where eventing is going

00:37:13.490 --> 00:37:16.070
as well as some really
cool demos showing

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:18.320
how eventing can be used today.

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:19.650
So definitely check that out.

00:37:19.650 --> 00:37:21.650
MARK CHMARNY: And maybe
one thing to add in here

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:24.500
is that, clearly,
this is beta product.

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:26.660
Cloud Run is still
in beta right now.

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:29.400
And Knative itself
is 0.5 release.

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:30.980
A lot of the use
cases that you're

00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:33.152
trying to address,
specifically on premise,

00:37:33.152 --> 00:37:34.610
are something that
we would be very

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:36.860
interested to understand
better and see

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:39.230
how Knative solves for those.

00:37:39.230 --> 00:37:42.770
So if you are at all dealing
with Kubernetes and serverless

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:45.320
seems like something
that Knative and Cloud

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:48.360
Run can help you with,
please, use some of those URLs

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:51.530
in here, including-- there
are some feedbacks on Twitter.

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:54.740
As well as Knative.dev
has some additional links

00:37:54.740 --> 00:37:58.010
you can use to give us some
ideas of what's missing

00:37:58.010 --> 00:38:01.310
or what else can
we add to Cloud Run

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:05.300
itself to make this more
appropriate for some of the use

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:06.920
cases you're trying
to solve with.

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:09.670
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

